---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Density from '@/components/tools/calc2/Density.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="density-calculator">
  <Density client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the density calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Density is mass per unit of volume. Enter two of mass, volume and density, and the density calculator
      finds the third, converts the units and shows whether the object floats in fresh water.
    </p>
    <pre><code>ρ = m ÷ V
m = ρ × V
V = m ÷ ρ
fraction under water = ρ ÷ 1,000 kg/m³   (when ρ is below 1,000)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A block of 750 g has a volume of 1 litre (0.001 m³). Its density is 0.75 kg ÷ 0.001 m³ = 750 kg/m³,
      or 0.75 g/cm³, close to oak. In water it floats with 75% of its volume under the surface, because it
      sinks until it displaces its own mass of water: 750 g, or 750 mL.
    </p>

    <h2>Units</h2>
    <p>
      1 g/cm³ = 1 g/mL = 1 kg/L = 1,000 kg/m³ = 62.43 lb/ft³. Specific gravity is the density divided by
      the density of water, so it has no unit.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The materials list gives typical values near room temperature; real wood, concrete and alloys vary by
      several per cent. Density changes with temperature, and for gases also with pressure. A hollow object
      floats or sinks by its average density, including the air inside.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
